WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.210
Some of the most memorable scenes in Hollywood
history were actually unscripted, adding a

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:10.080
flicker of authenticity to an otherwise artificial
— yet finely crafted — final product.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.180
These would-be "bloopers" were too good to
leave on the cutting room floor, so the directors

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.850
kept them in the final edit instead, where
they live on as rare glimpses of actors dropping

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.540
their masks, if only for a moment.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.020
Django Unchained

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.260
One of Django's chief aims in this 2012 Western
is to free his wife from the clutches of terrifyingly

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.560
crazed slave owner Calvin Candie.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:36.020
The meeting between Jamie Foxx's Django and
Leonardo DiCaprio's Calvin is extremely tense,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.880
and for dramatic effect, DiCaprio slams his
hand on a table … and right onto a glass.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.380
"Lay your palms flat on that tabletop.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.140
If you lift those palms off that turtleshell
tabletop, Mr Butch is gonna let loose with

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.120
both barrels of that sawed-off!"

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.820
DiCaprio didn't stop at all during the take,
even though he just seriously lacerated his

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.600
hand, nonchalantly picking out pieces of glass
and even lighting a cigarette.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:58.840
Blade Runner

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.200
Daryl Hannah took home a souvenir from the
set of Blade Runner in the form of a scar

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.000
on her arm … and the take in which she injured
it making its way into the movie.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.640
While playing a replicant named Pris, Hannah
ran from William Sanderson's genetic designer

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.280
J.F. Sebastian and slipped on the wet pavement
…

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.540
Breaking the window wasn't planned, but Hannah
finished filming the scene, preserving what

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.520
ended up being a fairly serious injury for
posterity.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.060
The Hateful Eight

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.660
For her role as the manipulative outlaw Daisy
in Quentin Tarantino's The Hateful Eight,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.560
Jennifer Jason Leigh had to know how to play
a little guitar.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.770
She learned on a vintage 19th century Martin,
on loan to the production from the Martin

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:42.950
Guitar Museum.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.979
In one scene, Leigh's co-star Kurt Russell
was supposed to yank the instrument out of

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:51.230
her hands and smash it to smithereens—but
the cameras were supposed to cut first, so

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.800
it could be swapped out for an ordinary guitar.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.100
"Music time's over."

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.020
"Whoa whoa whoa!"

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:04.980
But the cut didn't come, and Russell unknowingly
destroyed the antique Martin, valued at $40,000—so

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.180
Leigh's reaction is the real thing.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.480
"Whoa whoa whoa!"

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:10.920
True Lies

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.480
James Cameron's 1994 spy comedy contains some
of the biggest and most expensive sequences

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.950
in movie history, but the one scene from True
Lies everybody remembers two decades later

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.700
is Jamie Lee Curtis's striptease.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.580
Her character, Helen, takes off her clothes
for a man she believes to be an arms dealer,

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.060
but is actually her husband, Harry, played
by Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.600
Curtis's sexy moment is interrupted briefly
when she loses her balance …

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.500
It's a perfect pratfall with impeccable comic
timing, but it wasn't in Cameron's script.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.790
Curtis didn't want to do the scene the way
it was scripted, which called for her to get

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.180
completely naked and be filmed in silhouette.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.340
Cameron agreed to her changes, and they worked
out the choreography without Schwarzenegger's

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:54.840
knowledge.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.080
So that's a genuine reaction from Schwarzenegger,
who thought the fall would ultimately just

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.020
end up as a blooper.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.520
Midnight Cowboy

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.970
The 1969 low budget classic Midnight Cowboy
features one of the most famous unscripted

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:08.040
lines in movie history.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.560
As Dustin Hoffman's Ratso Rizzo and Jon Voight's
Joe Buck are walking across a very busy and

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.980
very real Manhattan street having a conversation,
a cab nearly hits Hoffman ...

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.580
"Hey, I'm walking here!

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.200
I'm walking here!"

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.420
Hoffman's response was so perfectly in character
that director John Schlesinger kept it in

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.500
the film, which went on to win Best Picture
at the Academy Awards.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:32.640
Annie Hall

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.930
It's a scene originally meant to serve as
just a transition allowing Woody Allen's proud

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.650
New Yorker character Alvy and his girlfriend
Annie to mention they're taking a trip to

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.700
Los Angeles — but it's become a classic
bit of physical comedy, thanks to an unplanned

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.200
moment.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.739
A stash of cocaine said to be worth $2,000
an ounce is passed around at a party, eventually

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:52.739
arriving at Alvy ...

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.300
"And what is the kick of it exactly, I never …"

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:04.020
Allen says it was "a complete, unplanned accident,"
but test screening audiences laughed uproariously

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.740
at it, so Allen, also the director of the
movie, kept it in.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:07.940
Thanks for watching!

